  "result": "`click.KeyValue` now works: `@click.option('--set', type=click.KeyValue())` turns `a=b` into `(\"a\", \"b\")`. All 9 new tests pass, and so does the full suite (1915 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed). The full run also reported 31,000 tests deselected; I didn't check where that filtering comes from. I couldn't run mypy because it isn't installed in this environment.\n\nHow it behaves:\n- The string is split on the first `=` only, so `a=b=c` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`.\n- An empty value is allowed: `a=` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`.\n- Input with no `=` (`novalue`), an empty key (`=v`, `=`) or an empty string fails with exit code 2 and the message `'novalue' is not a valid key=value pair.`\n- It also works with `multiple=True`, giving a tuple of pairs.\n\nThe class is in `src/click/types.py` and is exported from `src/click/__init__.py`. I also added it to `docs/api.md` and `docs/parameter-types.md`, and the tests are at the end of `tests/test_types.py`.\n\nOne change from the plan: `CHANGES.md` marks 8.5.0 as already released, so I added a new \"Version 8.6.0 \u2014 Unreleased\" section for the entry and used `versionadded:: 8.6` in the docstring. Change that if you're numbering the next release differently.\n\nNothing has been committed.",
